<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a serpentine conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A serpentine conveyor uses multiple parallel conveyor lanes connected by 180-degree turns to create a back-and-forth accumulation path. Products enter one end, travel through all lanes in sequence, and exit in FIFO order. Serpentine conveyors buffer products between machines that run at different speeds on <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/beverage/">beverage</a> and food packaging lines.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How much product can a serpentine conveyor hold?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Capacity depends on lane count, lane length, product size, and whether products run single file or several abreast. Worked example: a 10-lane table with 20-foot lanes has 200 feet (2,400 inches) of path. With cans about 2.6 inches across running single file, that holds roughly 920 cans. Running cans several abreast multiplies capacity by the number of cans across the lane. Buffer time equals capacity divided by the gap between upstream and downstream rates. At 1,000 cans per minute with the downstream machine stopped, 920 cans covers less than one minute. See our <a href="/conveyor-functions/storage-conveyors/">storage conveyor guide</a> for accumulation capacity planning.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between a serpentine conveyor and an accumulation table?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The terms overlap. A serpentine conveyor describes the back-and-forth lane pattern with 180-degree turns. An <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation table</a> is broader, covering serpentine layouts plus single-lane accumulators, bi-directional tables, and open mass flow surfaces. In beverage packaging, the two terms often describe the same multi-lane, reversing-path system.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">Do serpentine conveyors maintain FIFO product flow?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Yes. Serpentine conveyors with defined lane dividers maintain strict FIFO order. Every product follows the same sequential path through every lane. The first product in is the first out. Open mass flow tables without defined lanes do not guarantee FIFO. Use defined serpentine lanes for date-coded, perishable, or lot-traceable products.</p>

</div>
</div>
